  "primary_questions": [
    "Do shared shapes provide a useful and stable description of chemical or biological profiles?",
    "Does shape sharing improve held-out joint density and conditional prediction?",
    "Do locations and scales change while standardized skewness and correlation remain similar?"
  ],
  "interpretation_rules": [
    "All outcomes and variants are reported. No dataset, coordinate reference, component count or bound is selected using held-out performance.",
    "Three-acid and six-acid analyses have different sample spaces; their absolute log scores are not compared.",
    "The six-acid subcomposition retains exactly the six acids with strictly positive recorded measurements. The two acids containing instrument-limit zeros are included only in explicitly named eight-acid addition sensitivities. No post-fit variable selection.",
    "Olive folds are unstratified random folds. Leave-one-collection-area-out is a separate transport assessment using geographic metadata to define blocks, never to fit.",
    "Crab labels describe a balanced collection; fitted weights are sample fractions, not natural population prevalence.",
    "Crab geometric contrasts CW/FL, CW/BD and CL/RW follow the original study. Conditional BD prediction asks how well remaining dimensions predict body depth.",
    "The label-stratified nonparametric bootstrap preserves recorded sampling-group sizes. Its intervals measure conditional resampling stability; they are not asserted to have nominal coverage in weakly separated mixtures.",
    "Full-sample fits and bootstrap samples are descriptive; their test-array values are not held-out evidence.",
    "Shape sharing imposes a common standardized marginal distribution and common correlation matrix across latent components. It is a testable modelling restriction, not a biological mechanism.",
    "Primary application methods share five partitions. Bounds and nonstationary fits are always flagged. Package skew is replaced by the previously verified bounded comparator."
  ],
